# `debug`

`debug` captures a value into the run's [[error-reporting|structured log]] and
passes it on unchanged. It is the language's data capture: what a `print` would
be for, except that the value lands as a log entry with a severity, a label, a
span and its context, beside the errors and warnings of the same run.

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| debug(label = "books")
| map(c => c.metadata.pages)
```

The result is `[172, 412, 280, 95, 320, 310]`, exactly as without `debug`, and
the log gains one entry:

```text
debug[books]: Set<Card>, 6 elements
 --> shelf.hql:3:3
  |
3 | | debug(label = "books")
  |   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = value: [a-room-of-ones-own, dune, frankenstein, leaves-of-grass, the-common-reader, the-hobbit]
```

## Two spellings, one operator

Like every step, `debug` can be piped or called directly, and the two mean the
same thing:

```hql
xs | debug(label = "xs")
debug(xs, label = "xs")
```

The direct call is what makes it usable anywhere inside an expression, where it
logs once per evaluation:

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| map(c => debug(c.metadata.pages, label = "pages"))
```

```text
debug[pages]: 172   (map, element a-room-of-ones-own)
debug[pages]: 412   (map, element dune)
debug[pages]: 280   (map, element frankenstein)
…
```

Each entry's trace names the step and the element it came from, so a value
captured inside a lambda says which card produced it.

## Arguments

| Argument | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `label` | none | a name for the entry, shown in brackets and used to filter |
| `level` | `"debug"` | `"debug"` or `"info"` |

`level` stops at `info` on purpose. `debug` reports what a value *is*; it never
says something went wrong. A failure the program can recover from is a
`Result`, and an error the program cannot recover from is the language's to
raise — see [optional fields and optional values](../../../models/behavior/options-in-types-and-fields.md)
and [[error-reporting]].

## The entry

A `debug` entry has the same record shape as every other entry in the log:

```json
{
  "severity": "debug",
  "code": "debug",
  "label": "books",
  "message": "Set<Card>, 6 elements",
  "stage": "program",
  "span": { "start": 53, "end": 75 },
  "type": "Set<Card>",
  "found": ["a-room-of-ones-own", "dune", "frankenstein", "leaves-of-grass", "the-common-reader", "the-hobbit"],
  "trace": [{ "step": "filter", "position": 1 }, { "step": "debug", "position": 2 }]
}
```

`found` is the whole value as data; the rendered text shortens it, the JSON
never does.

## Rules

**`debug` is the identity on values.** Its result is its input, at the same
type. Removing every `debug` from a program cannot change what it returns, and
the checker treats `debug(x)` as having exactly the type of `x`.

**The program cannot read the log.** Capture flows one way, to the host. A
program whose result depended on what it had logged would no longer be a
function of its input, and every backend would have to agree on logging order to
agree on answers.

**Hidden is still captured.** The default shown level is `warning`, so `debug`
entries are invisible until asked for — `--report-level debug`, or
`level = "debug"` in the vault's `hql.toml`. Leaving a `debug` in a query costs
nothing a reader sees, and `--format json` returns every entry regardless of the
shown level.

**Order is deterministic.** Entries are ordered by their trace — step position,
then element — not by when a backend happened to evaluate them, so the log of a
parallel run matches the log of a sequential one.

Designed, not yet implemented. See [[error-reporting]] for severities, entry
context and configuration, and [[match-op]] for branching on what a capture
reveals.
